src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:16:whyItMatters: "Agentic handoffs fail silently when no durable object binds intent, evidence, and decision authority together; teams discover the gap only after a chain of confident-looking work turns out to answer the wrong question."
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:17:leaderTakeaway: "Make the Feature Contract the object of record, not the prompt or the final summary. Require explicit acceptance criteria, named evidence obligations, and a named decision authority before execution begins, and treat any change to those terms as a visible amendment rather than an implementation choice."
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:25:import WhereThisSits from '../../components/content/WhereThisSits.astro';
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:35:The research agent might have answered a subtly different question. The implementation agent might have interpreted "fixed" as "the test passes," while the product leader meant "the failure mode cannot recur." The reviewer could have examined the change without seeing the evidence behind the diagnosis. Nobody may know which source supported which claim, which acceptance criteria were binding, or who had authority to decide that the work was complete.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:39:"Unauthenticated" here does not mean that the agents lacked cryptographic identities. It means that the handoff did not carry an authoritative relationship among intent, claims, evidence, acceptance criteria, and decision rights. Each participant received a description of the work, but no durable object proved what the work was supposed to accomplish.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:41:The problem here isn't weak generation: it's broken continuity. Naming this failure mode is not a claim that <Term id="contract-as-spec">**contract-as-spec**</Term> solves every failure mode: bad evidence, mistaken reasoning, and a blind spot shared across a chain of verifiers stay real risks a contract does not by itself remove.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:43:Contract-as-spec is a method for preserving that continuity. It makes a <Term id="feature-contract">**Feature Contract**</Term>, not a prompt, ticket, plan, or final summary, the binding document for the work. The contract states the intended outcome, defines what completion means, and names the evidence required to support it, plus who can accept the result. Specialists can interpret and execute the contract, but they do not silently redefine it.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:48:**[Observed]** marks findings from a bounded landscape review or direct operating evidence. **[Proposed]** marks a method, interpretation, or product direction whose value should be validated in use. I did not invent this convention for the essay. It is the same instruction I give every agent I run, written down plainly: do not make things up; if you don't see it, don't say it.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:63:- <Term id="evidence-obligations">**Evidence obligations:**</Term> What must be inspected, tested, reproduced, or independently verified?
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:64:- <Term id="decision-authority">**Decision authority:**</Term> Who or what may accept the evidence and close the work?
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:66:These elements bind the stages together. A specialist does not just receive a task; it receives a task whose relationship to the intended outcome is explicit. A verifier does not just review an output; it evaluates named claims against named evidence. A decision-maker does not just approve a summary; it accepts or rejects a contract with a visible evidentiary state.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:92:A complete populated instance (claims bound to evidence, verifier status, a visible amendment, and the acceptance decision) is available as a [companion example](/essays/contract-as-spec-worked-example).
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:111:The conceptual core of contract-as-spec is a maturity model for evidence and authority. It distinguishes workflows that just produce outputs from workflows that can justify decisions.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:129:The workflow separates claims from narrative and binds each consequential claim to supporting evidence.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:135:A verifier tests whether the evidence genuinely supports the claim, whether contradictory evidence was considered, and whether the stated scope matches what was examined.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:137:Verification is not the same as agreement. It records the status of a claim: supported, contradicted, unresolved, or limited by an evidence gap.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:139:That distinction is also why test-based evaluation needs a stated boundary. SWE-bench evaluates models against issue-linked tests from real repositories, a valuable and demanding measure of whether a patch resolves a specified software problem.[^swebench] It is still evidence for that specified problem, not a blanket acceptance decision about every operational consequence a team may care about.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:155:**[Observed]** Stages four through six are still frontier territory at organizational scale. They are not imaginary, though. As of 2026-08-25, we run a small version at home every day: three different LLM vendors and deterministic scripts operate under one contract boundary, with durable daily run records showing the handoffs. That is lived practice at modest scale, not evidence that the institutional problem is solved.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:171:Can the planner point to the same acceptance criteria the verifier later evaluates? Can a claim be traced to its sources after the originating agent has disappeared? Does an approval identify exactly which evidence state was accepted? If an artifact is reused six months later, can its consumer determine which assumptions, validators, and contract version produced it? If a gate is deferred, can another operator resume it without reconstructing the decision from chat history?
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:175:**[Proposed, grounded in observed landscape coverage]** The meaningful differentiation of a governed agentic system lies in integration completeness rather than primitive technical novelty. Its value comes from closing the seams among intent, execution, evidence, verification, authority, and reuse.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:191:Before specialist execution, a gate can ask whether the contract is sufficiently clear to begin. Before accepting research, it can ask whether important claims have evidence and verification states. Before operational change, it can ask whether the acceptance criteria and risk boundaries have been met. Before reuse, it can ask whether the capability includes enough provenance to behave predictably outside its original context.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:205:This also changes the meaning of "blocked." A blocked agent is not just an agent that failed to continue; it can identify the exact contract obligation it cannot satisfy: unavailable evidence, ambiguous authority, contradictory acceptance criteria, or a task too large for the current specialist. That is useful state, and it lets the work be decomposed or escalated without erasing what has already been learned.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:217:A reusable artifact should answer: Where did this capability come from? What contract does it satisfy? Which evidence supported its acceptance? Which version is running? What changed between versions? What assumptions travel with it? Who may promote or retire it?
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:231:A research team can return claims with provenance. An engineering team can turn accepted claims into implementation criteria. A security team can add verification obligations and decision boundaries. An operator can inspect the evidence state without replaying the entire execution. A future team can reproduce the decision even if the original tools no longer exist.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:233:This does require discipline. Acceptance criteria must describe outcomes rather than activity, and evidence has to be durable enough to inspect later. Verification must be distinguishable from self-report. Decision authority must be explicit, and derived records need to identify their authoritative source.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:235:But the method does not demand maximal ceremony. Teams can begin with a small contract containing intent, three or four acceptance criteria, required evidence, and one named decision authority. They can add claim-level verification and reusable artifacts as the consequences of the work increase.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:239:I don't actually know whether anyone outside my own household would pay for this. The receipts in this essay show the mechanism operating in my home environment: specific handoffs, evidence states, and corrections I can point to; they don't prove a market, and I'm not going to pretend this essay settles that question. What I do have some confidence in, because I've watched it happen both ways, is what a chain of agents does without this discipline versus what it can do with it. The doubt is about who pays. The confidence is about what the mechanism does when you run it for real.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:247:Contract-as-spec makes those transitions explicit. It binds intent to acceptance criteria, routes bounded work through specialists, validates completeness at meaningful gates, and preserves the evidence needed to inspect or reproduce the result.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:251:<WhereThisSits slug="the-contract-is-the-work" />
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:323:Author: Nick Miethe. Thesis: human-originated. Evidence: operating observations plus a bounded internal landscape review (41 sources: 35 publicly linked primary sources and six non-public working materials; scope and caveats stated above). AI roles: research assistance, adversarial review, drafting. Human verification: final claim and source review by Nick. Research artifact: contract-as-spec landscape review, with its recovered source record listed above. Version: rev5. AI-involvement level: 3 (substantive synthesis and adversarial reasoning, thesis and evidence Nick's own operating observations).
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:20:leaderTakeaway: "Govern lineage, project binding, conditions of use, execution evidence, and acceptance as distinct but connected responsibilities. Do not equate a current registry record with a trustworthy run."
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:26:seoDescription: "A stale reviewer skill exposes why registries need a supply chain connecting artifact identity, conditions of use, execution evidence, and accepted outcomes."
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:33:import WhereThisSits from '../../components/content/WhereThisSits.astro';
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:51:  takeaway="Govern lineage, project binding, conditions of use, execution evidence, and acceptance as distinct but connected responsibilities. Do not equate a current registry record with a trustworthy run."
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:82:<div class="managed-variation" role="group" aria-label="Illustrative comparison of managed project variation">
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:126:| **Outcome** | Preserves evaluations or prior acceptance. Doesn't establish that this run fulfilled this request. | Connects output to intent, verification evidence, and the authority accepting or rejecting the result. |
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:153:I use <Term id="agentic-artifact-supply-chain">**agentic artifact supply chain**</Term> for the managed lifecycle of those reusable instructions, context, tools, policies and related assets. Existing work on agent supply chains and governed agent primitives already covers this ground; this is my organizing label for the lifecycle I'm building, not a new supply-chain analogy.[^primitives]
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:161:The lifecycle starts with **authoring**: the reviewer lives in one source repository, and a fix adds the missing guard. **Registration** makes that revision discoverable. **Approval** needs to attach to an exact revision and state its permitted use. The incident doesn't give me evidence of a complete approval record; it gives me a corrected source and a registry entry that didn't match deployment.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:173:At **use**, the project loads the deployed copy in its own context. The supply-chain obligation is to check that binding: the approved artifact, the expected context, and the allowed tools. The guard's presence is a prerequisite for the intended review, not evidence that the guard was followed.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:212:### The receipts that made managed variation concrete
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:220:Preserving Project B's patch keeps the evidence available, but that doesn't approve the patch for everyone. A useful local change can become a candidate for shared policy; it shouldn't automatically become shared policy simply because a sync command happened to encounter it. Project C's pin can travel the other way and become ineligible under a changed requirement. Both are policy questions in this example, not lab incidents I have recorded. What I want from the supply chain is that a shared correction reaches every destination that should have it, and stops at the one that shouldn't.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:233:I read these as validation of the package-management problem that sent me into this work. But convergence is uneven and component-specific. JFrog comes closest to joining the responsibilities in the reviewed scope; the MCP Registry deliberately remains a metadata service. The reviewed documentation doesn't establish a complete join from exact bytes through policy decisions and execution evidence to accepted outcomes for any of the four. That is a boundary of this comparison, not a claim about everything those companies can do.[^market-scope]
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:246:“Control plane” is the familiar shorthand. But an enterprise will run many governance systems, with different owners and enforcement mechanisms. <Term id="control-fabric">**Control fabric**</Term> is established terminology, including BCG's federated agent-control framing. Here it names independently operated systems that preserve compatible identity, authority, policy-decision and evidence records across handoffs.[^fabric] I use it to connect four responsibilities:
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:255:Four responsibilities, not four products, and they overlap: context is a governed dependency and an input to a runtime decision at once. Writing a policy under one of these headings doesn't establish that a runtime checks it, so I still need to name the mechanism in the execution path and what it blocks. My own <Term id="aos">Agentic OS (AOS)</Term> covers these unevenly. The table is the obligation, not a claim of coverage; they become a control fabric only once the handoffs carry identity and evidence.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:257:Shared identity means the deployment record and run record can refer to the same exact artifact. Shared evidence means the next system can inspect what the previous one established. An explicit handoff says who checks the next condition and what happens when it fails.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:261:Xia and colleagues' *Harnessing Agent Skills* describes a closely related architecture: it separates the stored artifact from skill-in-use, mediates execution, retains run evidence and feeds results back into managed supply. It also includes a run-scoped Skill-BOM.[^xia] That overlap helps make the comparison concrete. My contribution here is the architecture I'm implementing around shared corrections and deliberate local variation, the deployment checks it produced, and the failures those checks expose. The dated records below support that implementation history. They don't yet establish a general improvement in delivery or a unique architecture.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:273:Failure rate needs a denominator and a definition: failed reviews among comparable tasks, failure decided by an explicit acceptance criterion. A lower rate after deploying the guard would be useful evidence. It still wouldn't isolate the guard if the model or task mix changed too.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:277:I want to measure accepted outcomes with evidence. For this reviewer, that means a review that meets the request, with evidence of the required guard and an authorized acceptance decision. A <Term id="feature-contract">**Feature Contract**</Term> makes those obligations explicit; [The Contract Is the Work](/essays/the-contract-is-the-work/) takes up that boundary.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:288:A known source can supply a harmful artifact. Permitted work can still be wrong. A run using a skill does not establish that the skill caused its result. Fixed dependencies do not make probabilistic reasoning deterministic. The next essay considers the <Term id="deterministic-envelope">deterministic envelope</Term>, an existing way of describing constraints around probabilistic systems. My emphasis will be on controls outside model judgment and evidence that they mediate execution, alongside related work such as RAILS.[^envelope] Repeated successful reasoning can suggest a candidate for automation. It doesn't establish authority to promote it, or tell me when to withdraw it. Those remain questions to test.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:300:The same substitution showed up in provenance. In the September audit, SkillBOM emission was wired into bundle assembly. Deploying a single artifact used a different path and didn't automatically emit a BOM. Both operations could reasonably be described as deploying capability, but only one reached that evidence writer.[^bom-scope] A feature list saying “provenance supported” concealed the question I needed answered: supported on the operation I just performed?
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:338:    { label: 'Context and evidence homes', status: 'PARTIAL', detail: 'MeatyWiki and Research Foundry provide durable homes beyond the registry, while the audited provenance paths still have scoped gaps.' },
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:339:    { label: 'Loaded version to accepted outcome', status: 'PROPOSED', detail: 'The run-level relationship described above is a proposed evidence model, not a claimed implementation receipt.' },
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:344:## What I can prove today.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:350:[Read the full receipt set in Evidence Focus.](/essays/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain/evidence/)
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:373:<ClaimBadge kind="proposed" /> Those repairs support the managed-variation architecture in this essay. They don't establish a complete identity-to-outcome chain. I still need evidence for when a local improvement should become shared policy, when an automated fast path should return to judgment, and how an independent acceptance decision changes results. The three-project comparison is illustrative. No promotion, demotion or acceptance-by-assertion incident is being claimed from these receipts.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:403:<WhereThisSits slug="the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain" />
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:407:The reviewer didn't need a better name or another catalog. It needed the fix to reach the place that loaded it, a check that noticed when it hadn't, and evidence that the resulting review did what was asked. It also needed room for a project-specific improvement to survive the next shared correction. That's the bar I want the supply chain to meet when I'm no longer watching every project myself.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:425:[^xia]: Xia et al., [Harnessing Agent Skills: Architectural Patterns and a Reference Architecture for Skill-Mediated LLM Agents](https://arxiv.org/html/2606.20631v1), version 1, reviewed in the supplied September 17 research pack. Direct architectural overlap includes artifact/use separation, mediation, run evidence and feedback.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:430:[^workflow]: Lab implementation receipt: direction-aware project workflow synchronization, first committed August 11, 2026. Its source records project copies missing review checks alongside local copies carrying stricter conditional-verdict handling. The tool preserves project-specific rosters and refuses local-ahead overwrites; mixed changes require inspection. This is a separate incident from the stale reviewer, not evidence that every local difference is legitimate. Internal identifiers withheld; method only.
src/content/posts/governed-agentic-sdlc-01-productivity-paradox.mdx:27:import WhereThisSits from '../../components/content/WhereThisSits.astro';
src/content/posts/governed-agentic-sdlc-01-productivity-paradox.mdx:39:The METR 2025 randomized controlled trial on AI-assisted programming captures it precisely: experienced open-source developers using AI tools completed tasks 19% *slower*, despite self-reporting a perceived 20% speedup.[^metr] That's a 39-point perception gap between how fast they felt and how fast they actually were. They felt faster but delivered less. The tools compress time-to-first-draft while inflating time-to-verified-solution. That gap (subjective speed up, objective slowdown) isn't evidence that AI tools don't work. It's evidence of something more specific:
src/content/posts/governed-agentic-sdlc-01-productivity-paradox.mdx:114:The failure mode nobody puts on a slide deck. Your developers are already using AI tools you don't know about; spinning up private Claude sessions, pasting proprietary code into ChatGPT, running local Copilot instances outside your managed environment. Roughly 40% of organizations already have Shadow AI in active use. IBM Consulting's 2025 research on AI governance found 86% have zero visibility into how those tools interact with their data flows.[^ibm-governance] Not hypothetical. Current state.
src/content/posts/governed-agentic-sdlc-01-productivity-paradox.mdx:147:Consider what a typical gate-based governance model actually does: it creates synchronization points where human judgment is applied to human-produced artifacts. Code review gates assume the reviewer can hold the full context of a PR in their head. QA gates assume test coverage reflects intentional design decisions. Release gates assume that if the prior gates passed, the aggregate risk is managed.
src/content/posts/governed-agentic-sdlc-01-productivity-paradox.mdx:212:<WhereThisSits slug="governed-agentic-sdlc-01-productivity-paradox" />
