src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:103:It was mid-late 2025 when I started building <Term id="skillmeat">SkillMeat</Term>, my registry and deployment tool for reusable agent capabilities. Getting an agent to do something useful once was no longer the hard part. Making that behavior repeatable across projects, machines, teams, and runtimes was. Good agent behavior is a bundle: instructions, context, assumptions, examples, tools, permissions, and review expectations. When that bundle lived in one chat transcript or one local folder, it was fragile, and when another project needed it, the answer was usually copy and paste.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:138:Call that the <Term id="agentic-artifact">**agentic artifact**</Term>: whatever shapes how the agent reasons, acts, accesses tools, applies context, follows policy, or produces work.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:153:I use <Term id="agentic-artifact-supply-chain">**agentic artifact supply chain**</Term> for the managed lifecycle of those reusable instructions, context, tools, policies and related assets. Existing work on agent supply chains and governed agent primitives already covers this ground; this is my organizing label for the lifecycle I'm building, not a new supply-chain analogy.[^primitives]
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:246:“Control plane” is the familiar shorthand. But an enterprise will run many governance systems, with different owners and enforcement mechanisms. <Term id="control-fabric">**Control fabric**</Term> is established terminology, including BCG's federated agent-control framing. Here it names independently operated systems that preserve compatible identity, authority, policy-decision and evidence records across handoffs.[^fabric] I use it to connect four responsibilities:
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:255:Four responsibilities, not four products, and they overlap: context is a governed dependency and an input to a runtime decision at once. Writing a policy under one of these headings doesn't establish that a runtime checks it, so I still need to name the mechanism in the execution path and what it blocks. My own <Term id="aos">Agentic OS (AOS)</Term> covers these unevenly. The table is the obligation, not a claim of coverage; they become a control fabric only once the handoffs carry identity and evidence.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:271:<Term id="skillbom">**SkillBOM**</Term> already appears in research on skill identity, dependencies and run provenance.[^skillbom] Here it names this implementation's manifest for recording artifact composition and provenance. The useful question is which relationships it preserves. A manifest produced while assembling a bundle can identify the assembled artifacts without proving that a later session loaded them. A run record has to bind that use separately.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:277:I want to measure accepted outcomes with evidence. For this reviewer, that means a review that meets the request, with evidence of the required guard and an authorized acceptance decision. A <Term id="feature-contract">**Feature Contract**</Term> makes those obligations explicit; [The Contract Is the Work](/essays/the-contract-is-the-work/) takes up that boundary.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:288:A known source can supply a harmful artifact. Permitted work can still be wrong. A run using a skill does not establish that the skill caused its result. Fixed dependencies do not make probabilistic reasoning deterministic. The next essay considers the <Term id="deterministic-envelope">deterministic envelope</Term>, an existing way of describing constraints around probabilistic systems. My emphasis will be on controls outside model judgment and evidence that they mediate execution, alongside related work such as RAILS.[^envelope] Repeated successful reasoning can suggest a candidate for automation. It doesn't establish authority to promote it, or tell me when to withdraw it. Those remain questions to test.
src/content/posts/the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain.mdx:333:*A live run, redacted, September 15, 2026. The <Term id="task-completion-validator">task-completion-validator</Term> drift this section describes is recorded in the receipt below (August 6, 2026), not replayed here; that nonzero exit is a different, unrelated undeclared-copy finding.*
