<WhereThisSits slug="the-registry-wave-agentic-artifact-supply-chain" />

I place this work within the emerging discipline of <Term id="agentic-systems-engineering">**Agentic Systems Engineering**</Term>, already named in academic work by Sun and colleagues and in enterprise work by OutSystems.[^ase] This series examines my particular architecture through the systems I build and the failures they expose. Agentic SDLC is its application to software delivery. This reading path connects the questions; it isn't a one-to-one map of architecture layers.

The reviewer didn't need a better name or another catalog. It needed the fix to reach the place that loaded it, a check that noticed when it hadn't, and evidence that the resulting review did what was asked. It also needed room for a project-specific improvement to survive the next shared correction. That's the bar I want the supply chain to meet when I'm no longer watching every project myself.

## Sources

External claims below reflect researched, scoped status for this edition: each footnote states its own preview, GA, or tier boundary rather than treating verification as pending. Lab references identify supplied implementation receipts.

[^github]: GitHub Changelog, [Manage agent skills with GitHub CLI](https://github.blog/changelog/2026-04-16-manage-agent-skills-with-github-cli/). `gh skill` remains a public preview feature as of this edition.
[^jfrog]: JFrog, [Agent Skills Registry](https://jfrog.com/ai-catalog/skills-registry/) and [Skills Repositories](https://docs.jfrog.com/artifactory/docs/skills-repositories). The registry itself is generally available; artifact signing is optional and tier-dependent, and the locally enforced boundary described here remains a preview capability.
[^redhat]: Red Hat, [agentic catalog](https://catalog.redhat.com/en/ai) and [MCP catalog: discover, deploy, and connect](https://www.redhat.com/en/blog/mcp-catalog-here-discover-deploy-and-connect-red-hat-openshift-ai). Red Hat maintains the Agent Catalog and the MCP Catalog as separate roles, and both carry pre-GA boundaries as of this edition.
[^mcp]: Model Context Protocol, [The MCP Registry](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/registry/about). The registry is a preview metadata and discovery service, not an enforcement point.
[^drift]: Lab implementation receipt, August 6, 2026: global artifact-drift check, following a reviewer deployed five days behind its source. Method summarized from the supplied receipt; no claim of a measured reduction in review failures. Internal identifiers withheld; method only.
[^parity]: Lab implementation receipt, August 5, 2026: sibling instruction parity gate, following six weeks of stale instructions reaching one harness class. Internal identifiers withheld; method only.
[^staleness]: Lab implementation receipt, August 7, 2026: live-document scan for superseded operating-model assertions, with dated historical records exempted. Internal identifiers withheld; method only.
[^provisioning]: Supplied lab provisioning receipt (origin date unresolved): missing local artifacts are treated as unprovisioned; a tool-failure hook probes the registry and supplies a deployment command without writing the artifact itself. Internal identifiers withheld; method only.
[^coverage]: Registry implementation receipt, August 11, 2026: fleet-wide compliance scanning explicitly labelled target state. A local registration scan exists (August 24); that doesn't establish fleet-wide enforcement. Internal identifiers withheld; method only.

[^market-scope]: September 17 research comparison of the four documented product surfaces, supplied for this edition. Registry features, runtime mediation and adjacent provenance products were treated separately. No complete bytes-to-policy-to-execution-to-outcome join was documented in the reviewed scope; this is not a company-wide incapability claim. Each product's scope and preview boundary is recorded above.
[^primitives]: JFrog, [Managing AI Agent Primitives Like Real Software Packages with APM and JFrog](https://jfrog.com/blog/managing-ai-agent-primitives-with-apm-and-jfrog/), included in the supplied September 17 prior-art review. This supports the governed-dependency overlap, not a measured result for my systems.
[^xia]: Xia et al., [Harnessing Agent Skills: Architectural Patterns and a Reference Architecture for Skill-Mediated LLM Agents](https://arxiv.org/html/2606.20631v1), version 1, reviewed in the supplied September 17 research pack. Direct architectural overlap includes artifact/use separation, mediation, run evidence and feedback.
[^skillbom]: Alongside Xia et al., Liu, Wu and Li, [The Decomposition Is the Fingerprint: Per-Component Identity for Agent Skills](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.31272), and Jia et al., [Skills Are Not Islands: Measuring Dependency and Risk in Agent Skill Supply Chains](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.01136), use SkillBOM for related identity and dependency concerns. These are distinct schema meanings, not an established interoperability contract with this implementation.
[^fabric]: BCG, [Scaling Agents Without Creating Your Next Tech Legacy](https://www.bcg.com/ja-jp/publications/2026/the-four-pillars-cios-can-use-to-scale-agentic-ai), included in the supplied September 17 review for its federated control-fabric architecture. The term has older computing and identity uses as well; no first-use claim is intended.
[^envelope]: FlowX.AI, [RAILS](https://www.flowx.ai/research/rails), describes related deterministic-graph and stochastic-node architecture. The supplied review leaves exact publication chronology unresolved. The next essay's promotion and withdrawal questions are a research agenda, not a claim of deployed enforcement.
[^ase]: Sun et al., [Robust, Observable, and Evolvable Agentic Systems Engineering](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.20729), and [OutSystems' Agentic Systems Engineering announcement](https://www.outsystems.com/de-de/news/outsystems-agentic-systems-engineering-announcement), identified in the supplied prior-art review. These establish existing usage; earliest title and wording history remain outside this essay's claim.
[^workflow]: Lab implementation receipt: direction-aware project workflow synchronization, first committed August 11, 2026. Its source records project copies missing review checks alongside local copies carrying stricter conditional-verdict handling. The tool preserves project-specific rosters and refuses local-ahead overwrites; mixed changes require inspection. This is a separate incident from the stale reviewer, not evidence that every local difference is legitimate. Internal identifiers withheld; method only.
